<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Arabic is right-to-left. Every %s argument sits inside \u2068…\u2069
  (FSI…PDI) so an LTR value such as "1.2 GB" or a Latin subscription name stays
  one unit and does not flip the direction of the sentence around it. A
  sentence that would otherwise start with a Latin word (dns_ttl) opens with
  \u200F (RLM) so the line still reads right-to-left. StringsParityTest
  enforces both for every RTL locale.

  %d renders Arabic-Indic digits under this locale, so digits written into a
  string literally (traffic_daily_history) are Arabic-Indic too. Plurals carry
  all six CLDR quantities (zero, one, two, few, many, other).
-->
